  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Strong gusty winds today as a low\npressure system to the northwest moves into Oregon/Washington\nstate which is tightening weather gradients, forming a jet streak\nover Nevada. Strongest winds this afternoon are expected over\nHumboldt County, northern Elko County, and White Pine County with\ngusts reaching up to 40-50 mph, while everywhere else may see\ngusts up to 25-35 mph. Winds in combination with dry conditions\nresult in critical fire weather conditions. Red Flag Warning is in\neffect until 11 pm this evening as well as a Wind Advisory for the\naforementioned counties. Temperatures will still be several \ndegrees above normal this afternoon in the 80s with cooler \ntemperatures beginning to move in tonight, dropping the lows into \nthe 40s. \n\nSunday, breezy winds in the afternoon again with gusts reaching up\nto 25-35 mph (strongest winds along eastern Nevada). Elevated fire\nweather conditions during the day as dry conditions will leave\nhumidity below 15%, however remain borderline as when the winds\nincrease, humidity also rises above 20% due to the much cooler\ntemperatures moving in. Highs will be several degrees below normal\nfor northern Nevada in the 70s with central Nevada staying in the\n80s. Overnight lows will be coldest in northern Nevada in the 30s\nwith some areas dropping to freezing level, while central Nevada\ncools into the 40s. \n\nMonday, winds are expected to be light and fair as the main\nfrontal passage moves northeast along with the jet streak. A\nweak shortwave trough pattern will remain over the northwestern \nCONUS behind the low pressure system which will keep a near zonal \nto almost northwesterly flow over Nevada. This will keep \ntemperatures cooler across central and northern Nevada with highs \nin the 60s to 70s. Overnight lows will also remain cool with \nnorthern Nevada again in the 30s (some areas reaching to freezing \ntemperatures) and central Nevada in the 40s. \n\nTuesday and through the week, there is some inconsistencies \nwithin the forecast as to what transpires with the upper level \ntrough, with some models showing the trough strengthening to a low\npressure system and moving over Nevada, while others show the \ntrough remaining unchanged. The main consistency is that \ntemperatures will gradually warm back to near normal throughout \nthe week with highs staying in the upper 70s to 80s until reaching\nabove normal by next weekend. Winds will also be consistent \nstaying light to some mild afternoon breezes. No weather is \nforecasted throughout the week.",
